1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:05,130
Hello everyone. In today's video, we will perform UT using Cantata.

2
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:09,470
First, we have a list of C files that require UT.

3
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:15,300
To perform UT using Cantata, we will be provided with a database folder.

4
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:20,360
The database folder contains a header folder,

5
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:25,480
and the Cantata folders corresponding to the C files that require UT.

6
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:32,700
To perform UT for a specific module, copy the header folder

7
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:38,190
And the Cantata folder corresponding to the target C file into the workspace.

8
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:46,610
Take example, in this video, we will perform UT on the C file named Prc_Signal_Monitoring.

9
00:00:47,110 --> 00:00:53,150
We copy the header folder and the corresponding Cantata folder into the workspace.

10
00:00:55,750 --> 00:01:01,180
Then open Cantata and import these folders.

11
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:09,910
After the import is complete, we will implement Release.

12
00:01:15,910 --> 00:01:21,500
After the Release succeeds, we will implement generate the test script.

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:45,500
At this point, we can create the test cases needed to cover the C file.

14
00:01:46,790 --> 00:01:53,710
For example, I will create a test case to cover this function(PRC_Monitoring_HVSYS_V).

15
00:01:53,750 --> 00:02:00,380
Copy the function name, then generate a test case for that function.

16
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:08,260
The test case generated by Cantata has the following format.

17
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:11,640
The first section is Initialize Global Data.

18
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:18,910
This function lets Cantata assign values to global variables.

19
00:02:18,910 --> 00:02:27,600
We can also assign any required function inputs here.

20
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,170
Next is the Expected section.

21
00:02:30,170 --> 00:02:35,520
The Expected section contains the values we expect after the test case runs.

22
00:02:37,520 --> 00:02:40,840
Next is the Test Case Comment section.

23
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:49,100
Here, I describe the purpose of the test case and the cases it is intended to cover.

24
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:52,290
Next is the Expected Call section.

25
00:02:52,290 --> 00:03:00,480
All functions called by this test case are listed here.

26
00:03:04,480 --> 00:03:13,800
Next are the functions used for covering, and the check results section after having run the tool

27
00:03:13,800 --> 00:03:19,080
It will compare the expected result with the actual result.

28
00:03:19,080 --> 00:03:23,260
If they match, the test case result is Pass.

29
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:27,240
If they do not match, the test case result is Fail.

30
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:36,040
First, I will assign the required input values for the test case.

31
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:48,280
For example, this is a local variable, but its value comes from a global variable.

32
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:54,440
So I can assign the value directly to the global variable named RawSignal.

33
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:07,430
I need to cover the true case of this condition, so I set RawSignal to false.

34
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:13,060
I do the same for the conditions below.

35
00:04:14,060 --> 00:04:22,370
For CAN2 variable, this variable receives its value from the Message variable shown later.

36
00:04:22,370 --> 00:04:26,620
So I assign the value to that Message variable.

37
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:05,500
For the final condition, the condition checks the return value of this function.

38
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:14,780
We will perform stub on the value of this function, Cantata has supported this part.

39
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:24,800
I can modify the stub by searching for the function name in the test script.

40
00:05:27,300 --> 00:05:32,460
Here, I can create multiple stubs with different return values.

41
00:05:32,570 --> 00:05:38,310
The text in double quotation marks is the stub name.

42
00:05:38,310 --> 00:05:44,880
For example, I name one stub TRUE, and its return value is also TRUE.

43
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:48,900
I name another stub FALSE, and its return value is FALSE.

44
00:05:50,190 --> 00:06:01,220
Now, to cover the true case of this condition, I set this function's return value to TRUE.

45
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:12,730
Then I enter the name of the stub I just created.

46
00:06:12,730 --> 00:06:16,450
When the stub name is TRUE, the function returns TRUE.

47
00:06:21,450 --> 00:06:30,160
After assigning all these input values, the test case enters the true branch at line 78.

48
00:06:30,160 --> 00:06:35,550
The value of this local variable is then set to 0.

49
00:06:42,630 --> 00:06:50,750
Next, the code continues to line 90, where Cantata calls this function.

50
00:06:53,750 --> 00:06:58,770
Cantata also supports stubbing this function.

51
00:07:02,980 --> 00:07:06,880
At this point, I only need to check the call to this function.

52
00:07:12,690 --> 00:07:17,690
This function has two inputs: first is this Dem Event Parameter here.

52
00:07:17,690 --> 00:07:20,780
And second is the value calculated above.

53
00:07:20,780 --> 00:07:29,050
I can check the calculated value in this stub function.

54
00:07:29,050 --> 00:07:33,970
Because it is a local variable, I cannot check it directly.

55
00:07:33,970 --> 00:07:38,170
So I check it indirectly through the call to this CALL function.

56
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:16,200
As calculated earlier, this variable is set to 0.

57
00:08:16,200 --> 00:08:22,070
This corresponds to the enum fail condition detected.

58
00:08:29,070 --> 00:08:34,000
This call reaches the end of the function under test.

59
00:08:34,470 --> 00:08:38,090
Therefore, the test case is complete.

60
00:08:38,090 --> 00:08:43,970
Now I will fill in the test case comment.

61
00:08:48,970 --> 00:08:55,630
When writing the test case comment, I convert the formulas into text.

62
00:10:43,990 --> 00:10:48,040
After finishing the comment, I will run this test case.

63
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:32,000
The test case has finished running and result is Pass

65
00:11:32,840 --> 00:11:39,520
We can view the code coverage via the Coverage Viewer tab.

66
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:57,000
Here we can see that the execution matches the behavior described in the test case.

67
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,320
The return value also matches the expected value.

68
00:12:18,020 --> 00:12:26,100
Now, we will create additional test cases to cover all functions and conditions in this C file.

69
00:12:30,100 --> 00:12:38,430
After creating all required test cases, so that the C file reaches 100% coverage.

70
00:12:38,430 --> 00:12:41,290
Then we run the test cases again.

71
00:13:18,500 --> 00:13:21,980
In the summary, the test result is Pass.

72
00:13:21,980 --> 00:13:27,220
All lines of code in this C file are green.

73
00:13:27,220 --> 00:13:30,020
This means the entire C file has been covered.

74
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:36,650
We can view the code coverage percentage in the Coverage tab.

75
00:13:36,650 --> 00:13:45,910
The metrics here show 100% statement coverage.

76
00:13:45,910 --> 00:13:48,750
Decision coverage is also 100%.

77
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:51,950
MCDC coverage is also 100%.

78
00:13:51,950 --> 00:13:54,620
We can now view the report.

79
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:24,500
We can quickly check the report results in the Report Viewer tab.

80
00:14:24,680 --> 00:14:30,400
First, it shows the C file I tested.

81
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:32,810
The Summary status is Pass.

82
00:14:32,810 --> 00:14:35,390
The number of test cases executed.

82
00:14:37,010 --> 00:14:40,390
It also shows the coverage percentage achieved.

83
00:14:43,390 --> 00:14:48,000
I can also check the results via the folder.

84
00:14:51,220 --> 00:14:57,370
I will check the results via 2 report files.

85
00:14:57,370 --> 00:15:03,900
First one is the test report, whose interface is similar to what we saw in Cantata.

86
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:07,950
Second one is the test summary.

87
00:15:09,450 --> 00:15:16,280
It provides an overall view of the C file we ran.

88
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:19,690
The Overall Result is Pass.

89
00:15:20,390 --> 00:15:27,160
The coverage percentage is also 100%, as shown earlier.

90
00:15:29,160 --> 00:15:39,000
Once all Cantata test results reach 100% as the UT objective has been met.

91
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:46,500
This means we have completed coverage for this C file.

92
00:15:55,500 --> 00:16:02,000
We have also completed UT for this C file using Cantata.

93
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:05,800
Thank you for listening.